## Deployment

Kamal deploys via Docker. Hook samples live in `.kamal/hooks/`; copy and rename
a `.sample` file to activate it (e.g. `pre-deploy.sample` → `pre-deploy`).

### Git worktrees

Each linked worktree gets its own development and test databases: names in
[config/database.yml](config/database.yml) are suffixed with the worktree
directory name (override with `DB_SUFFIX`). A new worktree starts with no
databases, so run `bin/setup --skip-server` once in every new worktree before
anything else. Without `--skip-server` it ends by starting `bin/dev`. In a
worktree it also symlinks the gitignored `.env*` and `config/**/*.key` files
from the main checkout.

Remove a worktree only with `bin/worktree-teardown`, run from inside it. It
drops the worktree's databases, including the parallel test worker copies
(`_0`, `_1`, …), then
runs `git worktree remove` (extra arguments such as `--force` are passed on).

## Commits

- Work on a feature branch. Commit straight to `main` only when explicitly asked.
- Run `bin/ci` before every commit and ensure it passes (add
  `--with-system-tests` when the change can affect the UI).
- One logical change per commit. Never combine two features, or a feature and a
  fix, in the same commit.
- Keep commits small — up to roughly 100 significant lines. If larger, split
  the change into multiple sensible commits.
- The first line must be prefixed with one of `feat`, `fix`, `chore`, `docs`,
  depending on the purpose, and be no longer than 80 characters.
- The body is a bulleted list of changes, separated from the title by a blank
  line. It can be omitted for trivial changes.
